<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gwangmu Reform</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Gwangmu reforms</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gwangmu Reform was the modernisation programme of the Korean Empire, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">Emperor Gojong</a>'s era name Gwangmu, used from 1897 to 1907. Seeking to strengthen the state and the throne, the government surveyed land and issued land titles, founded schools, banks and factories, and introduced electricity, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>, a tramway and the first railway. The reforms were cut short when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1905.</p></div>
